EXAMPLE SEARCH COMMANDS

To perform these examples, shown indented below, simply hit <S>
(as in Search) at the Main Menu. The Main Menu is the first thing
displayed when you run the program. Or, if the program is already
running, you can return to the Main Menu by pressing <X> or <ESC>

The simplest search consists of a single word. You type the word, press
the Enter, or Return key and the list of verses where that word occurs
is displayed.
	amen

If you want to find where "love" and "hate" occur within the same
verse, we use a proximity of zero:
	love hate 0

If that last search didn't show enough verses we may want to widen
the search so that each word must occur within plus or minus 2 verses
of each other.
	love hate 2

You can restrict searches by book by using the @bookname
syntax. For example, to find all the occurances of "amen" in the book
of Revelation, search for:
	amen @rev 0

To search for the word "blessed" in the gospels:
	blessed @mat @mar @lu @joh , , , 0
or, this does the same thing:
	@mat @mar , @lu , @joh , blessed 0

The latter form uses slightly less computer memory space.
Here's a search where we don't want to look in a particular book.
It will look for all occurances of the word "food" without looking in
Genesis.
	food @gen -

Please note that there is a strict syntax that has to be followed if you
expect to get predictable results. There must be exactly one more operand
(word/book) in the search than there is operator (-/,/0).
